<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>Elephruit</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>ElephruitAppGroupIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(ELEPHRUIT_APP_GROUP)</string>
	<key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
	<string>$(MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET)</string>
	<key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
	<string>public.app-category.productivity</string>

	<key>NSHumanReadableCopyright</key>
	<string>Your data, on your Mac.</string>

	<!--
	  No usage-description keys are present, because no entitlement in this milestone triggers a
	  permission prompt. Each is written in the same commit as the feature that needs it, so a shipped
	  binary can never ask for access the user has not been offered a reason for.
	  See docs/06-privacy-and-entitlements.md.
	-->

	<key>NSApplicationSupportsSecureRestorableState</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSSupportsAutomaticTermination</key>
	<true/>
	<key>NSSupportsSuddenTermination</key>
	<false/>

	<!--
	  Sudden termination is declined on purpose: the editor debounces writes, and a process the system
	  may kill without notice could lose the last half-second of typing. Declining costs a few
	  milliseconds at quit and closes that window entirely.
	-->

	<!--
	  The `elephruit:` scheme, for deep links into the calendar and for waking the app after Safari
	  has already placed a confirmed web clip in the shared app-group inbox.

	  Registered now rather than in Phase 2's placeholder form because the calendar has links worth
	  making: a note, a Shortcut, or a bookmark can point at a particular day. The scheme reads only —
	  every calendar URL it accepts navigates. The clip URL carries no content and cannot choose what
	  is written; it only wakes the importer after the user pressed Save in the extension. The signed
	  extension has already written the immutable payload to the private app-group container.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>com.elephruit.Elephruit</string>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Viewer</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>elephruit</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>

	<key>UTExportedTypeDeclarations</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		  The type a work item carries while it is being dragged inside the app.

		  `UTType(exportedAs:)` means "this application is the authority on this identifier", and the
		  system checks that claim against this file at launch. It was not declared here, so every
		  launch logged `Type "com.elephruit.work-item-drag" was expected to be declared and exported in
		  the Info.plist of Elephruit.app, but it was not found` — and the type fell back to an
		  undeclared, dynamically-assigned identifier, which is not stable across launches.

		  Deliberately conforms only to `public.data` and claims no filename extension. It is a
		  private, in-process drag type: a task dragged into another application should carry nothing
		  that application can read, and a file on disk should never be mistaken for one.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.elephruit.work-item-drag</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>Elephruit Work Item</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.data</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<dict>
			<key>UTTypeIdentifier</key>
			<string>com.elephruit.archive</string>
			<key>UTTypeDescription</key>
			<string>Elephruit Archive</string>
			<key>UTTypeConformsTo</key>
			<array>
				<string>public.json</string>
			</array>
			<key>UTTypeTagSpecification</key>
			<dict>
				<key>public.filename-extension</key>
				<array>
					<string>json</string>
				</array>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Shown in the macOS permission prompt.

	  It used to say the app never writes. That was true of Phase D and is no longer: the calendar
	  module creates and edits events, which is the point of it. What replaces that promise is the
	  one a person deciding on this prompt actually needs — that what they write *about* a meeting
	  stays in Elephruit and is never put into an event other people can read. `EventDraft` is why
	  that is structural rather than a claim; see `CalendarWriteSafetyTests`.

	  `NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription` is the key that pairs with
	  `requestFullAccessToEvents()` on macOS 14 and later; the older `NSCalendarsUsageDescription`
	  pairs with the deprecated request and is deliberately not used.
	-->
	<key>NSCalendarsFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Elephruit shows your calendar and lets you create and change events. Your own notes about a meeting stay in Elephruit and are never added to a calendar event.</string>

	<!--
	  Shown in the macOS permission prompt for the People module.

	  Says what is read and, as importantly, exactly what can be written — because Contacts has a
	  single access tier and granting read access grants write access with it. The user cannot grant
	  less, so this string is where the app states the limit it holds itself to, and
	  `ContactsWriteSafetyTests` is where that limit is enforced.

	  It named a stricter promise until the People module learned to correct a linked contact: "only
	  reads your contacts and never changes them" was true then and would be a lie now. A permission
	  prompt that overstates the restraint is worse than one that states a real limit plainly, so this
	  now names what a change can touch and the two things — creating and deleting — that remain
	  impossible.
	-->
	<key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Elephruit can link the people you keep notes about to your existing contacts, so you do not type their details twice. It changes a contact only when you edit that person in Elephruit and confirm the change, and only their name, nickname, birthday, email addresses, phone numbers, websites, job title, department, and company. It never touches their photo, postal address, or notes, and it never creates or deletes contacts. Your own notes, reflections, and relationship history stay in Elephruit and are never written back.</string>

	<!--
	  Shown in the macOS permission prompt for the Tasks module.

	  Unlike the calendar and the address book, this integration genuinely writes: a task manager
	  linked to Reminders that cannot tick a reminder off is not linked to anything. So the string
	  says so plainly, and says what stays private — because that is the half people cannot verify
	  for themselves.

	  `NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription` is the key that pairs with
	  `requestFullAccessToReminders()` on macOS 14 and later. EventKit offers no read-only or
	  write-only tier for reminders, so full access is the only request there is; the older
	  `NSRemindersUsageDescription` pairs with the deprecated request and is deliberately not used.
	-->
	<key>NSRemindersFullAccessUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Elephruit can show your Reminders lists alongside your own tasks, and keep linked reminders up to date in both directions. It only touches reminders you link, and only when you ask. Areas, projects, people, notes, and everything else private to Elephruit are never written into a reminder.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
